Let $F$ be a field, $q\in F^{	imes}$, $d,n\ge1$, and let $S=S(d,n)$ be the q-Schur algebra with Weyl modules $W^{\lambda}$ for $\lambda\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$ ([[def-type-a-q-schur-algebra-and-weyl-modules]]). Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional right $S$-module and let $(a_{\lambda})_{\lambda\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)}$ be integers such that $$\dim_F igl(M\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}} igr)=\sum_{\lambda\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)}a_{\lambda}\,\dim_F igl(W^{\lambda}\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}} igr)$$ for every partition $
u\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$, where $\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}}$ are the diagonal semistandard basis elements ([[def-type-a-q-schur-algebra-and-weyl-modules]]). Then $[M]=\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}[W^{\lambda}]$ in the Grothendieck group $G_0(S	ext{-mod})$ ([[def-grothendieck-group-of-an-essentially-small-abelian-category]]). Consequently equality of all semistandard weight-space dimensions forces equality in the Grothendieck group.

items.strategy:
**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 Exactness of a weight projection. Fix an idempotent $p=p_
u\in S$. For a short exact sequence $0	o A	o B\xrightarrow{f}C	o0$ of right $S$-modules, the sequence $0	o Ap	o Bp	o Cp	o0$ is exact as vector spaces. Injectivity and the kernel identity follow from the underlying sequence and $a=ap$ for $a\in Ap$. For surjectivity, lift $c\in C$ to $b\in B$; then $f(bp)=cp$. Right multiplication by $p$ is a linear projection, and preserves every $S$-submodule, although it need not be an $S$-linear endomorphism when $p$ is not central. Taking dimensions proves additivity. Only the canonical partition weight projections are used. [given, F1, algebra]

2.1 Weight functionals on composition series. Apply step 1.1 to every short exact sequence of a finite composition series of $N$. Its factors are the simple modules $L^\mu$, so $$\dim_F(Np_
u)=\sum_\mu[N:L^\mu]\dim_F(L^\mu p_
u).$$ The defining relations of the Grothendieck group similarly give $[N]=\sum_\mu[N:L^\mu][L^\mu]$. This argument requires exactness on vector spaces, not the false assertion that every noncentral weight projection lies in $\operatorname{End}_S(N)$. [step 1.1, F1, F3]

3.1 Invertibility of the weight matrix: put $X_{\mu
u}:=\dim_F(L^{\mu}\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})$ for $\mu,
u\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$. Feeding a composition series of $W^{\lambda}$ into step 2.1 gives $$K_{\lambda
u}=\dim_F(W^{\lambda}\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})=\sum_{\mu}d_{\lambda\mu}X_{\mu
u},$$ that is, $K=dX$ with $d=(d_{\lambda\mu})$ and $K=(K_{\lambda
u})$. Both $d$ and $K$ are unitriangular over $\mathbb Z$ by [F1] and [F2] respectively, hence invertible over $\mathbb Z$, and therefore $X=d^{-1}K$ is invertible over $\mathbb Z$. [step 2.1, F1, F2]

4.1 Conclusion: write $c_{\mu}:=[M:L^{\mu}]$ and $b_{\mu}:=c_{\mu}-\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}d_{\lambda\mu}\in\mathbb Z$. By step 2.1 applied to $M$ and to the identity $\dim_F(W^{\lambda}\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})=K_{\lambda
u}$, for every $
u$ $$0=\dim_F(M\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})-\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}\dim_F(W^{\lambda}\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})=\sum_{\mu}c_{\mu}X_{\mu
u}-\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}\sum_{\mu}d_{\lambda\mu}X_{\mu
u}=\sum_{\mu}b_{\mu}X_{\mu
u}.$$ Since $X$ is invertible over $\mathbb Z$ by step 3.1, $b_{\mu}=0$ for all $\mu$. Hence $c_{\mu}=\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}d_{\lambda\mu}$, and step 2.1 together with the defining relations [F3] gives $$[M]=\sum_{\mu}c_{\mu}[L^{\mu}]=\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}\sum_{\mu}d_{\lambda\mu}[L^{\mu}]=\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}[W^{\lambda}].$$ [step 2.1, step 3.1, F1, F3]

5.1 Consequence: if two finite-dimensional right $S$-modules $M,N$ satisfy $\dim_F(M\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})=\dim_F(N\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})$ for all $
u\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$, then applying the argument of step 4.1 to the difference of their multiplicity vectors shows $\sum_{\mu}([M:L^{\mu}]-[N:L^{\mu}])X_{\mu
u}=0$ for all $
u$, so the invertibility of $X$ gives $[M:L^{\mu}]=[N:L^{\mu}]$ for all $\mu$ and hence $[M]=[N]$ in $G_0(S	ext{-mod})$. [step 3.1, step 4.1, F3] ∎
